@divider num="vi–ix" kind="MODULES" title="Translation, Context,/Fault Lines" sub="How translation decisions get made, the eight questions, the four kinds of disagreement, and the household verse worked all the way through" foot="EIGHTEEN PAGES"

@page id="m-trans" rh="LEARNING THE BIBLE · TRANSLATION · 1 OF 5" m="INFORMATION; MASORETES; NA28 · UBS5; I.4"
# Translation, and how to compare it
lede: The old title asked which translation to trust. It's the wrong question, because it implies one exists that never needs checking. The goal is to understand how translation decisions get made, so you can see one being made and say what it did.
k: Three languages
rec:
Hebrew | Most of the Old Testament. Written originally without vowels. The vowel points were added centuries later by the Masoretes, scribes preserving how the text was read aloud, so a consonantal text can sometimes be voiced more than one way, and where it can, translators choose.
Aramaic | Parts of Daniel and Ezra, and the everyday speech of Judea in the first century. Jesus taught in it, and the gospels record him in Greek.
Greek | The New Testament, in Koine, the common Greek of the eastern Mediterranean. It isn't uniform: Mark's is rough and Hebrews' is polished. Translations that flatten every book to one register lose that.
k: Manuscripts and variants
No original of any biblical book survives. What exists is thousands of copies, and they differ, overwhelmingly in spelling, word order and obvious slips, and occasionally in something substantive. Textual criticism reconstructs the earliest recoverable text from that evidence. A few famous passages are missing from the earliest manuscripts, and reputable Bibles say so in a footnote: the longer ending of Mark (16:9–20), the woman caught in adultery (John 7:53–8:11), and the Trinitarian clause in 1 John 5:7–8.
k: Where differences actually come from
Most differences between translations aren't ideological. They come from a genuinely ambiguous word, a construction with no clean English equivalent, a choice of register, or a choice between manuscripts. Some are theologically motivated, and those are worth finding, but if you assume every difference is, you won't be able to spot the real ones.
box: You don't need the languages
You need to know where the decisions live, and to look at more than one Bible when a sentence is doing something to somebody.
/end

@page rh="TRANSLATION · PHILOSOPHY AND APPARATUS · 2 OF 5" m="KJV · NASB · ESV; NIV · NRSV · CSB; NLT; THE MESSAGE"
# The spectrum
lede: It isn't a scale from honest to dishonest. It runs from keeping the structure of the original to keeping its effect on the reader, and every point on it gives something up.
tabrl:
| Approach | What it prioritizes | What it gives up |
| Interlinear | Word-for-word correspondence, original order under the line | Readability. A study tool, not a Bible. |
| Formal ("literal") · KJV, NASB, ESV | The original's grammar and ambiguity, kept even where English strains | Clarity in places. An ambiguity preserved is still one you have to resolve. |
| Middle · NIV, NRSV, CSB | Accuracy in natural English | Some seams |
| Functional ("dynamic") · NLT, Good News | What the sentence does, in idiomatic English | Where the original was ambiguous, the translator has already chosen for you |
| Paraphrase · The Message | Immediate impact, often one author's voice | Any claim to precision. Good for hearing a passage fresh, useless for an argument about wording. |
| Committee or solo | Committees give consistency, individuals give voice. Committees have institutional homes, traditions, funders and audiences. | Neither is neutral, and neither is disqualified. Knowing who made it is part of reading it. |
k: Why the footnotes matter more than the translation you chose
A footnote that says "or," "other manuscripts read," or "Greek uncertain" is the translator telling you a decision happened here. When a verse is being used against someone, check whether the page admits an alternative.
k: Chapters, verses and headings aren't scripture
Chapter divisions came in the thirteenth century, verse numbers in the sixteenth, and section headings from modern editors. They make the text findable and quotable, and they also impose breaks the author didn't write. Quoting a verse is normal. The question is whether the unit you quoted is doing the work of the unit around it.
prompt: Who authorized the Empire's translation, and what its footnotes are allowed to say | fill

