@page rh="TRANSLATION · WHEN TO GO OUTSIDE · 5 OF 5" m="LEXICON; COMMENTARY; BDB · HALOT · BDAG"
# Four triggers
lede: Comparing English Bibles gets you a long way. These four situations mean stop and find a real source, a lexicon, a commentary or a scholar, before it goes into the novel as a claim.
box: I · The argument rests on one word
If a whole doctrine hangs on the range of a single term, the range is the question, and English can't answer it. The household verse is in exactly this situation.
/end
box: II · The translations split along denominational lines
When the pattern of who renders it which way matches the pattern of who teaches what, something besides grammar is operating, and you need to be able to name it exactly.
/end
box: III · A footnote says the text is uncertain
Take the admission seriously. The manuscripts disagree or the syntax won't resolve, and building a scene on the resolved version is building on a choice.
/end
box: IV · You're about to say what the original "really" means
The most dangerous sentence available to a non-specialist, and one used with total confidence by people on every side. A character may say it. The companion may not, without a source.
/end
prompt: The claims about the original languages I've been repeating without checking | fill

@page id="m-context" rh="LEARNING THE BIBLE · READING IN CONTEXT · 1 OF 3" m="METHOD; I.5"
# Eight questions
lede: Run all eight, in order, before deciding what a passage means. They won't make you an expert, but most confident misuse of scripture happens by skipping one, and skipping is easy to catch in yourself if you have a list.
rec:
1 · What kind of text is this? | Genre first, always. It decides what the passage can establish before any other question is worth asking.
2 · Who's speaking? | The narrator, a character, God as quoted, a rival, a friend who turns out to be wrong. Job's comforters are in the Bible and they're mistaken, and the book says so.
3 · To whom? | A congregation with a particular problem, a king, a foreign nation, one person. Applying an oracle addressed to Babylon to yourself is a category error with a long history.
4 · What comes before and after? | Read the paragraph, then the chapter. Most weaponized verses only work once the sentences on either side have been cut.
5 · What world produced it? | Empire, occupation, exile, monarchy, the household economy, the legal status of women and slaves. The setting is often the subject.
6 · What do the words do? | Command, describe, lament, promise, threaten, concede, argue. Compare two translations and see whether they agree about which.
7 · Who gains from this reading? | Ask it of every reading, including the one you prefer. A reading that benefits you isn't wrong because of it, and it's worth checking twice.
8 · What does this reading cost? | And who pays. POSSIBLE: The Empire has never asked this one out loud, and Eden eventually can't stop asking it.
box: What the method buys you
It doesn't buy authority over people who quote this book for a living. Many of them run these questions better than you will. It buys the ability to tell a reading that has done the work from one that skipped a step, including your own, and to say which step.
/end

