@page id="m-fault" rh="LEARNING THE BIBLE · THE FAULT-LINE MAP · 1 OF 4" m="CONTESTED; I.6"
# Eight fault lines
lede: The eight places where reading this book divides people, and where the Empire has a doctrine. Research won't produce agreement on any of them, but it reliably rules out weak claims and shows what the argument is actually about.
tabrl:
| Fault line | What's actually in dispute | Where TKC uses it |
| Headship | The range of one Greek noun, the reach of the mutual-submission verse, and what carries over from a Roman household code | The Binding. Every household in the Empire. |
| Women speaking | Restriction passages in the Pauline letters set against the undisputed letters, which name women as coworkers, a deacon and an apostle. Authorship and dating carry weight here. | The Vowed. Who may teach, and who may only recite. |
| Slavery | Texts that regulate slavery without abolishing it, and the principle by which a tradition later called it wrong. The verses aren't in doubt. The principle is the argument. | Designation and labor tracks. Who can be assigned. |
| Divine violence | Conquest narratives and judgment oracles: history, theological rhetoric, later reflection or ancient war convention, and how any of it fits a God described elsewhere as merciful | The Fall. Every purge called cleansing. |
| Obedience to rulers | Romans 13 against the prophets, Revelation and the movement's own martyrs. General principle, or advice to a vulnerable congregation in the imperial capital? | The Empire's most quoted text. Its whole legitimacy. |
| Wealth | Whether the warnings target attachment, structural injustice or wealth itself, and how they sit beside the prosperity language of the wisdom books | Crownward theology. Blessing as evidence of favor. |
| Purity and the body | Which rules are which, and by whose framework. The ritual and moral distinction is a later category applied to the texts, not a label printed in them. | Designation exams, the Salt Ceremony, fertility review |
| The end of things | Apocalyptic read as address to its own moment, as a future program, as a recurring pattern, or as liturgy | Whose reading is orthodoxy, and whose is sedition |
prompt: The fault line my novel stands on hardest | fill

@page rh="CONTESTED READINGS · FOUR KINDS OF DISAGREEMENT · 2 OF 4" m="CONTESTED; METHOD"
# Not all contested alike
lede: The most important distinction in this part. Treating all four as equally open is how a companion becomes useless, and how a well-read person ends up unable to say that something is simply false.
box: I · Serious scholarly dispute
Specialists with the languages, the manuscripts and the historical training disagree, and each side can state the other's evidence. The range of *kephalē*. The authorship of Ephesians. Represent both, and don't pick a winner in the companion's own voice.
/end
box: II · Denominational or traditional disagreement
Communities differ because of their confessions, their governance and inherited practice, more than because the evidence is contested. Who may be ordained. Whether Revelation is read aloud. Name the tradition instead of treating its position as the neutral one.
/end
box: III · Open theological questions
Questions the text raises and doesn't close, where evidence runs out and judgment begins. What the conquest narratives say about God. Whether Job is ever answered. These stay open honestly, and they're where a novel does its best work.
/end
box: IV · Unsupported claims
Claims that fail on the evidence: invented etymologies, a word supposedly mistranslated in every Bible, a council that decided something it never discussed. These are wrong, and the companion can say so, while noting that a character may believe one sincerely and build a life on it. The curse of Ham is in this category (Genesis dossier).
/end
box: The sorting question
Before writing any dispute into a scene, ask which of the four it is. Category I earns two credible characters. Category IV earns one character who's wrong and one who knows it, and that's a different scene entirely.
/end
prompt: The category IV claim the Empire teaches as settled fact | fill

