@page id="m-house" rh="LEARNING THE BIBLE · THE HOUSEHOLD VERSE · 1 OF 6" m="EPH 5.18–6.9; P46 · VATICANUS; SINAITICUS · ALEXANDRINUS; METZGER, TEXTUAL COMMENTARY; GURRY, NTS 2021; I.7"
# Ephesians 5
lede: The worked example, because the Empire's household law is built on this passage and because nearly everything hard about reading scripture is in it at once: a disputed author, a supplied verb, a contested word, a Roman legal background, and a verse break in the wrong place.
k: Where the sentence actually starts
Verse 21 tells the whole congregation to submit to one another. Verse 22 addresses wives. In P46 and Codex Vaticanus, verse 22 has no verb at all, and Clement of Alexandria quotes it the same way, so the instruction to wives borrows the participle of verse 21, and the two verses are one grammatical movement. Codex Sinaiticus, Alexandrinus and most later manuscripts supply a verb, and most English Bibles supply one too, with a section heading printed between the verses. The heading is modern apparatus, not scripture.
corr: It listed Sinaiticus among the verbless witnesses. Sinaiticus has the verb (*hypotassesthōsan*). The verbless witnesses are P46 and Vaticanus.
contested: What the participle settles, and what it doesn't
The grammatical link isn't in dispute. What it implies is. One reading holds that mutual submission in 21 governs everything after it. Another holds that 21 introduces submission in general and 22 onward specifies its ordered forms. Bruce Metzger's committee preferred the verbless text, and Peter Gurry (New Testament Studies, 2021) argues the verb is original. The grammar alone doesn't decide the meaning, and anyone who says the Greek settles it is overselling, in either direction.
/end
k: The authorship question
Ephesians is one of the disputed letters. Whether Paul wrote it divides scholars, which affects any argument that runs "the same author who names women as coworkers also said this."
box: The structural lesson
Quote verse 22 alone and you have a rule for wives. Quote 21 through 33 and you have a passage that gives husbands three times as many verses (nine to three) and about 116 Greek words to the wives' 40 (Mowczko). INTERPRETATION: The mechanism isn't mistranslation. It's fragment selection, plus a heading, plus teaching the doctrine before anyone reads the paragraph. Any institution with a lectionary and a catechism can do it without altering a word.
/end

@page rh="THE HOUSEHOLD VERSE · ONE WORD · 2 OF 6" m="KEPHALĒ; BEDALE 1954; GRUDEM, TRINJ 1985; FITZMYER 1989; CERVIN 1989; BDAG"
# Head
lede: The Greek word is *kephalē*. Its range in this period is one of the most argued questions in New Testament lexicography, and the dispute is genuine on both sides.
tabrl:
| Proposed sense | The case for it, and the strain against it |
| Authority over | Well attested for rulers and governing bodies, and the natural reading if the passage is arranging a hierarchy. Wayne Grudem surveyed 2,336 instances (1985) and argued "source" is rare, and Joseph Fitzmyer (1989) agreed. Strain: the passage defines the husband's headship by self-giving and nourishing, and gives husbands no order anywhere. |
| Source or origin | Attested in some contexts, a river's head, a source of supply, and fits the body imagery, where the head supplies and the body grows. Stephen Bedale (1954) began the modern debate with it, and Catherine Kroeger and Philip Payne developed it. Strain: how common this sense was in ordinary first-century use is exactly what's disputed. |
| Preeminence | Foremost, most prominent, first in honor, with no command attached. Richard Cervin's reply to Grudem (1989) is usually grouped here. Strain: honor and authority are hard to separate in a society organized around both. |
| Metaphor, unresolved | The writer is working an extended body metaphor and didn't intend an institutional definition, which would explain why readers have pulled incompatible institutions out of it for two thousand years. |
contested: The word rendered "submit"
*Hypotassō* means to arrange oneself under someone. The middle voice is often read as something the subject does herself, and scholars disagree about how much weight that can bear. INTERPRETATION: A voluntary act inside a legal system where the alternative is ruin is voluntary only in a limited sense. POSSIBLE: The Empire understands this perfectly. It never forces. It makes the alternative unlivable.
/end
box: What not to do with this page
Don't use it to announce that the Greek has been solved and the hierarchy dissolved. The point is to be able to say which sense a reading assumes and what that assumption costs.
/end
prompt: Which sense the Empire's doctrine assumes, and whether its Prelates know there's a choice | fill

@page rh="THE HOUSEHOLD VERSE · THE ROMAN BACKGROUND · 3 OF 6" m="ARISTOTLE, POLITICS I; BALCH, LET WIVES BE SUBMISSIVE (1981); EPH 5.25–33; EPH 6.5–9"
# The household code
lede: Wives, children and slaves, addressed in that order, isn't an invention of this letter. It's a recognizable Greco-Roman form, instruction on managing a household, which was a political unit as much as a domestic one, because the ordered household was understood as the foundation of the ordered state.
k: What the form assumes
A male head with legal authority over wife, children and enslaved people, the household as an economic enterprise, and the state's interest in its order. Aristotle's *Politics* (Book I) sets out the three pairs. A first-century reader would recognize the shape at once, and would notice the departures.
contested: Where this version departs from the form
It addresses the subordinate parties directly as moral agents. It gives the husband the longer and harder instruction. It grounds the arrangement in Christ's self-giving and not in nature or the head's prerogative. Whether that amounts to subverting the code, a Christianized version of it, or an accommodation that made the movement socially survivable is a live argument, and all three are held by people who've done the work. David Balch argued that 1 Peter's code helped present the movement as respectable to suspicious outsiders.
/end
k: The instructions to husbands
Love as Christ loved and gave himself up. Nourish and cherish as your own body. The passage gives husbands no command to rule, enforce or correct. INTERPRETATION: A doctrine of headship that produces enforcement is drawing on the noun in verse 23, and not on anything the passage tells husbands to do.
k: The part nobody quotes at weddings
The same code goes on to enslaved people and their masters (6:5–9). An institution that treats the wives section as timeless and the slavery section as historically conditioned is making an interpretive decision it usually declines to defend. Noticing that is the most useful single move available to a character inside such an institution.
prompt: How the Empire handles the slavery section of the same passage | fill

@page rh="THE HOUSEHOLD VERSE · WHO READS IT HOW · 4 OF 6" m="COMPLEMENTARIAN; EGALITARIAN; DANVERS 1987; CBE 1987; PART IV"
# Four positions
lede: Held by real people with real arguments. Learn to state each one in its own terms before deciding anything. A character who can only caricature the other reading hasn't thought about it yet.
tabrl:
| Position | What it holds, and on what evidence | Its hardest problem |
| Ordered roles (complementarian) | Distinct and permanent roles: the husband leads, the wife submits, both equal in worth. Rests on the plain sense of headship in the era's usage and on the order of the code itself. | Nothing in the passage authorizes enforcement, and verse 21 addresses everyone |
| Mutual submission (egalitarian) | Verse 21 governs the whole section, and the household instructions apply a shared obligation. Rests on the grammar of the participle and the content of the husbands' instructions. | The instructions are still asymmetrical, and only wives are told to submit |
| Historically situated | A first-century congregation taught to live inside an existing legal structure without being destroyed by it. Rests on the household-code parallels and the letter's occasional character. | It needs a principle for deciding what carries over to other centuries, and stating that principle is hard |
| Accommodation | A movement softening its radical edge to survive scrutiny. Rests on the trajectory from the undisputed letters toward the Pastorals. | It depends on authorship and dating questions that aren't settled |
small: The modern American debate took its current shape in 1987, with the complementarian Danvers Statement and the founding of Christians for Biblical Equality. See Part IV.
prompt: Assign one position to each character who has an opinion, then one to a character who's never been told there's more than one | fill

@page rh="THE HOUSEHOLD VERSE · THE EIGHT QUESTIONS, RUN · 5 OF 6" m="METHOD, WORKED; EPH 5.21–6.9"
# The method, worked
lede: The eight questions, answered on this passage, so you can see a completed run before doing your own.
rec:
1 · Kind of text | A letter containing a household code: correspondence, not legislation, with a recognizable moral-instruction form inside it.
2 · Speaker | The letter's author, writing in Paul's name. CONTESTED: whether Paul wrote it. Not a narrator reporting, and not God quoted directly.
3 · Audience | A congregation, or several, in the Roman province of Asia. Wives, husbands, children and enslaved people are all addressed as present and listening.
4 · Before and after | Before: mutual submission, addressed to all. After: husbands, then children and parents, then slaves and masters. The wives' instruction is the shortest unit in the sequence.
5 · The world | Roman legal households with a male head, an economy that included slavery, a state invested in domestic order, and a small movement under intermittent suspicion.
6 · What the words do | Instruct, in some manuscripts through a participle hanging on the previous sentence. One contested noun. The husbands' portion is longer and makes the heavier demand.
7 · Who gains | From the fragment: whoever holds household authority, and any state that needs orderly households. From the whole passage: much less clear, which is why the fragment is what gets printed.
8 · What it costs | Under the fragment reading, a woman's standing depends on a man's character, with no recourse written into the text. Ask who in Aurelia pays that, and what she's told when she says so.
prompt: Where my own first run of this passage went wrong | fill

